# Runtime dependencies: the packages the panel imports directly. EDIT THIS FILE, then regenerate
# requirements.txt from it (run in python:3.10-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --unsafe-package=dnspython \
#     --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
#
# requirements.txt is pip-compile's lockfile: the WHOLE resolved set, every package these pull in,
# each pinned to an exact version AND to the hashes of its published files. Pinning only the direct
# dependencies left the rest to float. install.sh skips pip when requirements.txt is unchanged, and
# `pip install -r` never upgrades a package that already satisfies a range. So a security fix in a
# transitive package (Werkzeug, python-engineio, Jinja2, ...) never reached an installed host.
# Dependabot proposed no bump for a package it could not see, and pip-audit checked a freshly
# resolved set that no host ran. With every package locked, a bump to any of them changes
# requirements.txt: install.sh re-runs pip, Dependabot has something to propose (grouped pip
# updates, reviewed), and pip-audit reads the set that is deployed. tests/unit checks that the lock
# stays closed: each package's own requirements, for CPython 3.10-3.14 on Linux, are pinned too.
#
# The hashes: CI, the fuzz image and Lighthouse install with `--require-hashes --only-binary :all:`,
# so a file that is not byte-for-byte what was locked is refused. install.sh passes no flag and gets
# the same check anyway, because pip turns hash checking on whenever a requirement carries a hash.
# It must NOT pass the flag: an update can land an older pinned commit whose requirements.txt has
# no hashes, and with the flag that install fails.
#
# Compiled on 3.10, the oldest Python the panel supports (Ubuntu 22.04's python3): Dependabot
# re-runs pip-compile on the Python the lockfile's header names. Resolved on 3.14 it picks bidict
# 0.24 and SQLAlchemy 2.1, which need 3.11 and cannot install on 22.04. One lock serves 3.10-3.14
# because the resolved set is the same on each, and every pin ships wheels for each. No environment
# markers here: a 3.10 compile drops a requirement whose marker is false on 3.10, so a package
# needed only on a newer Python must be listed bare (tests/unit refuses a marker in this file).
#
# dnspython is left out (--unsafe-package; Dependabot reads the flag back from the header and keeps
# it). eventlet declares it for eventlet.support.greendns, its DNS resolver written in Python, and it
# is the only package here that does. The panel turns greendns off instead (EVENTLET_NO_GREENDNS=yes,
# set before eventlet is imported: panel/install/app.py and panel/__init__.py) and resolves names
# with the operating system's own resolver on a worker thread (panel/core/green_dns.py). That keeps
# one dependency, and its advisories, off every host: dnspython's fixes now need Python 3.11, which
# Ubuntu 22.04 does not have. Without --allow-unsafe on purpose: with it, pip-compile pins the
# unsafe package anyway. Because eventlet's metadata still names dnspython, every install of this
# file passes --no-deps (the file is the whole closure apart from that one) and then runs pip check,
# which may report only "eventlet ... requires dnspython, which is not installed." (install.sh
# _pip_check_deps; CI runs the same function). tests/unit (part06, part193) holds all of this.
flask
flask-socketio
flask-login
flask-sqlalchemy
flask-wtf
pyotp
qrcode
paramiko
bcrypt
eventlet
cryptography
websocket-client
